WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.725
Finally, the package.

2
00:00:01.725 --> 00:00:04.738
An agency never authorizes
a cloud service;

3
00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:07.750
it authorizes its own
system, which uses one.

4
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:10.875
The provider's certification
is evidence you read.

5
00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:13.007
Your package is evidence you write,

6
00:00:13.007 --> 00:00:17.125
and its plan should contain only
what the agency is responsible for.

7
00:00:17.125 --> 00:00:19.384
FedRAMP's Rev5 guidance is blunt:

8
00:00:19.384 --> 00:00:23.483
don't copy and paste provider
controls into the agency plan,

9
00:00:23.483 --> 00:00:29.088
and don't blindly copy provider findings
into your plan of action and milestones.

10
00:00:29.088 --> 00:00:31.763
The program writes the boundary down once,

11
00:00:31.763 --> 00:00:36.275
as a boundary record the system owner
keeps, and every artifact cites it.

12
00:00:36.275 --> 00:00:40.900
The system description names the boundary
the authorizing official determined.

13
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.225
The external services
section names each certified

14
00:00:44.225 --> 00:00:47.475
service with its Marketplace
package, class and SKU.

15
00:00:47.475 --> 00:00:50.918
Each crossing, such as a
federated trust or a connector,

16
00:00:50.918 --> 00:00:53.825
is an information exchange
with an agreement.

17
00:00:53.825 --> 00:00:57.213
Gaps you'll close go on the
plan of action and milestones.

18
00:00:57.213 --> 00:01:00.812
Each equivalent is described
as a component in OSCAL.

19
00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:03.903
When the probe finds a
change, one record changes,

20
00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:06.762
and every narrative that
cites it is current.

21
00:01:06.762 --> 00:01:08.413
Know whose package you're reading.

22
00:01:08.413 --> 00:01:13.515
The GCC listing says the service leverages
Azure Government as its infrastructure,

23
00:01:13.515 --> 00:01:17.962
so inheritance runs through two provider
packages before it reaches you.

24
00:01:17.962 --> 00:01:21.716
And on October 21, 2026, that
listing's trust-center and

25
00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:27.176
secure-configuration fields were empty,
so record where you got the guidance.

26
00:01:27.176 --> 00:01:31.699
A compensating narrative takes four
sentences: what the control needs;

27
00:01:31.699 --> 00:01:35.686
what the provider documents as
unavailable, with the citation;

28
00:01:35.686 --> 00:01:40.363
what runs instead, marked derived; and
where the remaining gap is recorded.

29
00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:43.465
The system owner builds this
before the package reaches

30
00:01:43.465 --> 00:01:46.750
the authorizing official, and
keeps it current from then on.
